_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 25
---
order: 25
---
short_label: III.25
---
statement: Given a segment of a circle, to describe the complete circle of which it is a segment.
---
red_highlight: yes
---
proof:

Let *ABC* be the given segment of a circle.

It is required to describe the complete circle belonging to the segment *ABC,* that is, of which it is a segment.

Bisect *AC* at *D,* draw *DB* from the point *D* at right angles to *AC,* and join *AB.* [!just I.10; I.11]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="230" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.25a diagram" src="propIII25a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.25a",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;60,30",
            "C;point;free;60,200",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;midpoint;AC",
            "DB;line;perpendicular;D,A;0;0;0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;DB,30,115",
            "E;point;circumcenter;A,B,C;black;green",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E",
            "ABC;sector;sector;E,A,C;0;0;black;random",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "ACE;polygon;triangle;A,C,E;0;0;0;background"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

The angle *ABD* is then greater than, equal to, or less than the angle *BAD.*

First let it be greater.  Construct the angle *BAE* on the straight line *BA,* and at the point *A* on it, equal to the angle *ABD.*  Draw *DB* through to *E,* and join *EC.* [!just I.23]

Then, since the angle *ABE* equals the angle *BAE,* the straight line *EB* also equals *EA.* [!just I.6]

And, since *AD* equals *DC,* and *DE* is common, the two sides *AD* and *DE* equal the two sides *CD* and *DE* respectively, and the angle *ADE* equals the angle *CDE,* for each is right, therefore the base *AE* equals the base *CE.* [!just I.4]

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="150" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.25b diagram" src="propIII25b.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.25b",
        pivot: "D",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;120,30",
            "C;point;free;120,170",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;midpoint;AC;black;green",
            "DB;line;perpendicular;D,A",
            "B;point;last;DB",
            "ABC;sector;arc;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

But *AE* was proved equal to *BE,* therefore be also equals *CE.*  Therefore the three straight lines *AE, EB,* and *EC* equal one another.

Therefore the circle drawn with center *E* and radius one of the straight lines *AE, EB,* or *EC* also passes through the remaining points and has been completed. [!just III.9]

Therefore, given a segment of a circle, the complete circle has been described.

<hr width="30%">

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_2" width="250" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.25c diagram" src="propIII25c.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_2",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.25c",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;200,30",
            "C;point;free;200,200",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;midpoint;AC",
            "DB;line;perpendicular;D,A;0;0;0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;DB,30,115",
            "E;point;circumcenter;A,B,C;black;green",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "ABC;sector;sector;E,A,C",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "ACE;polygon;triangle;A,C,E;0;0;0"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

And it is manifest that the segment *ABC* is less than a semicircle, because the center *E* happens to be outside it.

Similarly, even if the angle *ABD* equals the angle *BAD* and *AD* being equal to each of the two *BD* and *DC,* the three straight lines *DA, DB,* and *DC* will equal one another, *D* will be the center of the completed circle, and *ABC* will clearly be a semicircle.

<hr width="30%">

But, if the angle *ABD* is less than the angle *BAD,* and if we construct, on the straight line *BA* and at the point *A* on it, an angle equal to the angle *ABD,* the center will fall on *DB* within the segment *ABC,* and the segment *ABC* will clearly be greater than a semicircle. [!just I.23]

Therefore, given a segment of a circle, the complete circle has been described.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
<br clear="all">
---
body:

The construction in this proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
